/* text.yon — the package file of `text`, under prelude/.
 *
 * The site C(Txt): the category of text.
 *
 *   Objects  String — the place of this directory. The compiler counts it.
 *   Arrows   the `morphism` below, the generators of the site.
 *
 * The topos name is not written (it comes from the file). The terminal is
 * not declared (retired: every topos has one by definition).
 *
 * On the name. The space is called `text` (the fused primitive code), the
 * place is called `String` — and the fusion `String -> text` has been live
 * in the compiler since June. It is not an inconsistency: `text` is the
 * name of the code in the kernel, `String` the declared face. In the toml
 * the world is `Txt`.
 *
 * Careful on macOS: the filesystem is case-insensitive, so this file and a
 * hypothetical `Text.yon` would be one and the same — an `rm Text.yon`
 * deletes the package (it already happened, and no gate noticed: the site
 * stood there with no generators). Today the text place is `String`, so
 * nothing collides; if a `Text` place is ever born, rename this file
 * first.
 */
topos where {

  /* ─── The generator — only one ───────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * `concat` is the single operation everything else descends from:
   * substring, reverse, take, skip, replicate and index_of are built from
   * concat + char_at + from_char, so they are derived and live in the
   * place. A functor obtains them by functoriality — F(g∘f) = F(g)∘F(f)
   * leaves no choice.
   *
   * Putting an arrow here costs: every functor leaving Txt has to
   * translate it. One generator is the cheapest site there can be.
   *
   * And `concat` is the cleanest non-commutative monoid in the prelude:
   * associative, with "" as its neutral, and concat(a,b) != concat(b,a).
   * A good test bench for the algebra cantiere — the prelude's other
   * monoids (Additive on Number, Multiplicative on Int64) are all
   * commutative.
   *
   * `uses Additive` arrives with the algebra cantiere: the position does
   * not exist in the grammar today.
   */
  morphism concat(a: String, b: String): String
}

/* ═══ The Reverse functor — and it is contravariant ═════════════════════
 *
 * `reverse` is not merely an involution: it is an antihomomorphism on
 * concat.
 *
 *     reverse(concat(a, b))  =  concat(reverse(b), reverse(a))
 *                                             ^^^^^^^^^^^^^
 *                                             the order swaps
 *
 * So it is not a covariant functor: it is contravariant, that is a functor
 * `Txt -> Txt^op`. It is the language's first example of contravariance,
 * and the antihomomorphism of inversion is the classical case in algebra
 * (the same structure as (a·b)^-1 = b^-1·a^-1 in groups).
 *
 * It says more than the `morph Reverse` in String.yon: that one declares
 * the involution, this one also declares how it acts on concat.
 *
 * The form is designed, not live — two pieces are missing: `on object` /
 * `on morphism` on a functor (today it has parameters and a single body)
 * and a way to say contravariance. Three roads, Antonio's decision:
 *   (a) a nameable opposite world (`Txt^op` / `op Txt`)
 *   (b) a word on the functor (`functor Reverse contravariant from ...`)
 *   (c) a law that declares it (`law antihomomorphic`), grammar unchanged
 *       — the least invasive, and coherent with "law is the one word for
 *       obligations"
 * Below it is written in form (c), the most conservative.
 *
 *   functor Reverse from Txt to Txt {
 *     on object(s: String): String { return String.reverse(s) }
 *     on morphism concat via concat_flipped
 *     law involutive          // reverse(reverse(s)) == s
 *     law antihomomorphic     // reverse(a ++ b) == reverse(b) ++ reverse(a)
 *   }
 *
 *   // the image of concat under Reverse: the arguments swapped. That is
 *   // what makes the square commute — and the fact that a "flipped" is
 *   // needed at all is the contravariance, made explicit.
 *   fun concat_flipped(a: String, b: String): String {
 *     return String.concat(b, a)
 *   }
 *
 * ═══ The other functor — genuine, blocked on two decisions ═════════════
 *
 *   functor MapChar from Txt to Txt        [blocked]
 *     on object(s: String): String { ...apply f to every character... }
 *     on morphism concat via concat
 *     law additive     // map(f, a ++ b) == map(f, a) ++ map(f, b) — true
 *
 * This one is covariant and its law is true: applying a function to every
 * character distributes over concatenation. But it wants (1) the `Char`
 * place — today `char_at` returns a Number, so a character-to-character
 * function has no type to name; (2) the encoding decided (byte or
 * codepoint) — on which what a character even is depends. Both on the
 * ledger.
 *
 * ═══ And the two that are not written — the shield comment ═════════════
 *
 * It would feel natural to declare `Txt -> Num` (parse) or `Num -> Txt`
 * (formatting). Neither is a functor, and the counterexamples are
 * arithmetic:
 *
 *   formatting (Num -> Txt), not additive:
 *       to_text(2 + 3)            = "5"
 *       to_text(2) ++ to_text(3)  = "23"      !=
 *
 *   parse (Txt -> Num), not additive:
 *       parse("1" ++ "2")         = 12
 *       parse("1") + parse("2")   = 3         !=
 *
 * Concatenating strings and adding numbers are operations that do not
 * correspond: neither preserves the monoidal structure. As functors they
 * would exist on objects only, with no algebraic law — that is, carrying
 * no guarantee, and with `law additive` they would be lies.
 *
 * So `parse` and `from_int` stay `fun` in the places: they are arrows
 * between objects, not between categories. It is not laziness — it is that
 * the functor would declare something false.
 *
 * This is the place where someone will try again. The comment stays.
 *
 * ─── Ledger ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * `List<String>` for `split`: it wants the prelude's containers (today
 *     `List<T>` is a language generic, not a declared place).
 * The functor laws are verified by nobody (`ft_laws` is read only by the
 *     formatter): for the language's first contravariant functor, the
 *     verification is what would give the contravariance its meaning.
 * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
